        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Indoor Heater for Power Outage",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-indoor-heater-for-power-outage/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the safest indoor heater for a power outage?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The safest options burn nothing at all: cold-rated sleeping bags, layered clothing and a heated blanket on a power station. For actual room heat without electricity, choose a propane heater the manufacturer rates for indoor use, such as the Mr. Heater Buddy series, which has an oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over shutoff. Always pair it with a battery-powered CO alarm and the ventilation its manual requires."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is the Mr. Heater Buddy really safe to use indoors?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Mr. Heater markets the Buddy series as indoor-safe, and the heaters include an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff. That makes indoor use reasonable when you follow the manual: provide the specified fresh-air opening, keep the stated clearance from combustibles, use 1-lb cylinders indoors, and run a working battery CO alarm in the same room. Many safety agencies advise turning it off before sleeping."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a 1-lb propane cylinder last in a Buddy heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 1-lb cylinder holds a little over 21,000 BTU of energy. At 9,000 BTU an hour that works out to roughly two to two and a half hours, and at 4,000 BTU about five hours. The 3,800 BTU Little Buddy runs about five to six hours. Cold weather and partly used cylinders shorten that, so stock more cylinders than the math suggests."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I run a space heater on a portable power station?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "You can, but not for long. A 1,500-watt heater drains a 1kWh station in roughly 35 to 40 minutes, and even a low setting only stretches that to about an hour. That energy is better spent on a heated blanket, which can run most of the night on the same battery. Space heaters make sense on generators or large whole-home batteries."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I heat my house without electricity?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Concentrate on one small room. Close it off, block drafts, cover windows and gather everyone there. Use an indoor-rated propane heater with a CO alarm and the required ventilation during waking hours, and let cold-rated sleeping bags and layered clothing do the work overnight. An existing, inspected wood stove or fireplace is also a good option if it is in sound condition."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use my gas oven or stove to heat the house?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Gas ovens and ranges are not designed as space heaters, and running one for hours with the door open can produce dangerous levels of carbon monoxide and creates a fire risk. The same goes for camp stoves and grills of every kind. Use only heaters labeled for indoor residential use, and keep a battery CO alarm working whenever anything burns inside."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Should I buy a kerosene heater for power outages?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "We left kerosene heaters off this list. Whether they may be used indoors depends on state and local rules, they require careful fuel storage and refueling outdoors, and they need generous ventilation. For most households, an indoor-rated propane heater with 1-lb cylinders is simpler to store and use safely. If you consider kerosene, check your local codes and the manual first."
