          Most winter outage deaths are not from cold. They are from carbon monoxide, produced by people trying to stay warm with the wrong thing. The right emergency heat plan is a carbon monoxide alarm, a heater the manufacturer rates for indoor use, one closed-off room, and a lot of blankets. Everything else is detail.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-heat-fails" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Your Heat Stops When the Power Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every modern furnace needs electricity, even if it burns natural gas or propane. The igniter, the control board, the inducer fan that vents exhaust and the blower that moves warm air through the ducts all run on household current. When the grid goes down, a gas furnace goes cold just as surely as an electric heat pump. The same is true of most boilers, whose circulator pumps and controls need power, and of pellet stoves, which need electricity for the auger and fans.
          </p>

          <p>
            That leaves three ways to stay warm. You can restore power to the heating system itself, which is what a properly installed generator or home battery with a transfer switch does. You can bring in a separate heat source that does not depend on the grid, which in practice means an indoor-rated propane heater or a wood stove you already have. Or you can reduce how much heat you need by closing off a room, insulating people instead of air, and protecting the parts of the house that freeze first.
          </p>

          <p>
            The first option is the most comfortable and the most expensive. A furnace blower typically draws a few hundred watts with startup spikes above that, which is within reach of a large power station or a mid-size generator connected through an inlet and transfer switch by an electrician. Our guide to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators for power outages</a> covers sizing, and the <a href="/best-home-battery-backup/">best home battery backup</a> systems can run a gas furnace quietly if they are wired to that circuit.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide is about the second and third options: what to do when you do not have whole-house backup, or when the outage outlasts it. The goal is modest and specific. Keep one room in the 60s, keep people warm enough to sleep, keep the pipes from freezing, and do it without filling the house with carbon monoxide. Every recommendation here is built around that goal, and anything that fails the last part is not on the list.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
