        <div id="propane-rules" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Indoor Propane: The Rules That Keep It Safe
          </h2>

          <p>
            A propane heater is only indoor-safe if the manufacturer designs, tests and labels it for indoor use, and only if you use it the way the manual says. The Mr. Heater Buddy models carry two safety devices that make that possible. An oxygen depletion sensor, usually called an ODS, shuts off the burner if oxygen in the room drops below a set level. A tip-over switch kills the flame if the heater is knocked over. Heaters sold for patios, garages, job sites or ice fishing often lack one or both, and they are not substitutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Understand what the ODS does and does not do. It responds to low oxygen, not to carbon monoxide directly. A properly working burner with enough fresh air produces very little CO, and the ODS is there to shut things down before the air in a sealed room gets bad enough for combustion to become incomplete. It is a good safety device. It is not a carbon monoxide alarm, and it is not a reason to skip one. Dust, damage and altitude can all affect how a sensor behaves, which is another reason for a separate alarm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ventilation is not optional, even for indoor-rated heaters. The manuals call for a fresh-air opening of a minimum size, which in practice means cracking a window or a door to the outside by the amount specified for your heater and setting. It feels wrong to let cold air in on purpose. Do it anyway. The heater consumes oxygen and produces water vapor and carbon dioxide, and a small, steady supply of outside air is what keeps the room safe to breathe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearance and placement matter as much as ventilation. Keep the heater on a hard, level floor, away from foot traffic, at least the distance the manual specifies from curtains, bedding, furniture and anything else that can burn, and out of reach of children and pets. Do not sleep with a heater running unless the manual explicitly allows it, and even then many safety agencies advise against it. Turn it off before bed and let the sleeping bags take over.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fuel handling is the last piece. Use 1-lb disposable cylinders inside, connect and disconnect them outdoors where possible, and check connections for leaks with soapy water rather than a flame. Do not bring a 20-lb barbecue tank into the living space; fire codes generally prohibit storing or using full-size cylinders indoors, and the Buddy hose-and-filter setups are not a loophole around that. Store spare cylinders outside, out of direct sun, and never in a basement. We have left kerosene heaters off this list because whether they may be used indoors depends on state and local rules, and their fuel handling and ventilation demands are higher.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="co-alarms" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Carbon Monoxide Alarms: Buy This First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Carbon monoxide is colorless and odorless, and early symptoms of poisoning feel like flu: headache, dizziness, nausea, tiredness. People sleeping cannot notice them at all. CO poisoning reliably spikes after major winter storms and outages, almost always because a generator, grill or other fuel-burning device was run inside or too close to the house. An alarm is the only thing that reliably catches it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The problem is that many homes rely on hardwired CO alarms, and some of those have no battery backup. When the grid fails, they go silent. Check yours. If the alarm does not have a battery, or the battery is years old, buy battery-powered alarms like the First Alert CO400 and put them where the risk will be: in the room with the heater, in or near each sleeping area, and on every level of the house. Combination smoke and CO alarms work too, as long as they run on batteries.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test alarms before the season and replace batteries on schedule. Every CO alarm also has a service life printed on it, after which the sensor is no longer reliable; replace the whole unit when it expires. If an alarm sounds, do not try to find the source. Turn off the heater if you can do so immediately, get everyone outside into fresh air, and call 911 or the fire department from outside. Do not go back in until responders say it is safe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think of the alarm as the gate for everything else on this page. If you do not have a working battery-powered CO alarm, you should not run any fuel-burning heater indoors, no matter what the label says. With one in place, an indoor-rated propane heater used according to its manual is a reasonable, widely used emergency heat source. Without one, it is a gamble you do not need to take.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Electric Heat on a Power Station: The Hard Math
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power station seems like the obvious answer to an outage, and for phones, lights, a fridge and a CPAP it is. For heat it is mostly the wrong tool. Electric resistance heaters are 100 percent efficient at turning electricity into heat, but heat simply takes a lot of energy. A standard space heater draws 1,500 watts on high. A 1kWh power station holds about 1,000 watt-hours, and after inverter losses delivers something closer to 850 or 900. Divide, and the heater runs for roughly 35 to 40 minutes.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Runtime on a 1kWh Power Station (Approximate)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~35 min</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">1,500W space heater on high</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~1 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">750-900W heater on low</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~15 hr</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">50W heated throw</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Days</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Phones, lights and a router</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Turning the heater down helps less than people expect. A low setting of 750 to 900 watts roughly doubles the runtime to about an hour. That is barely enough to take the chill off a room before the battery is empty, and the room cools again within an hour of the heater stopping. Even a large 4kWh home-backup station only buys a few hours of space heating, which is energy you probably wanted for the refrigerator and medical equipment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heating people instead of rooms changes the numbers completely. A heated throw or blanket typically draws in the range of 50 to 100 watts, so the same 1kWh station runs one for most of a night, and a larger unit runs two or three. Electric mattress pads, heated vests with USB power banks and 12V heated blankets for cars all work on the same principle. If you are shopping for a station with winter in mind, our guides to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power stations</a> and the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best power stations for home backup</a> show which sizes cover the fridge, the CPAP and a couple of heated blankets together.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want electric space heat during outages, plan for a generator rather than batteries. A generator running safely outside, at least 20 feet from doors and windows, can carry a 1,500-watt heater for as long as you have fuel. A power station charged by solar can keep low-watt loads going indefinitely, as our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator guide</a> explains, but winter sun rarely refills a battery fast enough to feed a space heater. Spend the stored energy where it goes furthest: blankets, lights, and the devices that keep people informed and safe.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warm-room" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. The One Warm Room Plan
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most effective thing you can do in a cold-weather outage is to stop trying to heat the whole house. Pick one room, ideally a smaller interior room on the side of the house away from the wind, with few exterior walls and windows. Move everyone into it. A Portable Buddy rated for about 225 square feet can make a closed bedroom comfortable; it has no chance with an open-plan first floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Close the doors to every other room and block drafts under them with rolled towels. Hang blankets over windows and over doorways without doors, and draw heavy curtains. Close the curtains on north-facing windows, but open south-facing ones during sunny hours to collect free heat, then close them before dusk. A tent or blanket fort set up inside the room, especially over a bed, holds body heat remarkably well and is something children often enjoy rather than endure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dress for the room rather than heating the room for your clothes. Wool or synthetic base layers, a fleece, a warm hat and dry socks matter more than a few degrees of air temperature. Cotton is a poor choice because it holds moisture. Eat regular meals and drink warm drinks if you can heat water safely, because the body generates heat by digesting food. Keep moving during the day; sitting still in a cold room is when people get chilled.
          </p>

          <p>
            At night, let bedding carry the load. One cold-rated sleeping bag per person, a pad or mattress underneath, and a heated throw on a power station for anyone who runs cold is a complete plan for the sleeping hours. Turn the propane heater off before bed, check that the CO alarm is working, and run it again in the morning when everyone is awake. Keep a good battery light handy so nobody needs a candle; our guide to the <a href="/best-emergency-lantern/">best emergency lanterns</a> covers models that run for days on a charge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Medical equipment changes the priorities. Anyone using a CPAP, oxygen concentrator or other powered device needs that load covered first, and heat second. A CPAP without its heated humidifier draws little enough to run for several nights on a mid-size station, and our <a href="/best-battery-backup-for-cpap/">CPAP battery backup guide</a> covers how to size it. If a long outage and severe cold combine with medical needs, the right move is often to relocate to a warming center or a relative with power.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Protecting Pipes When the House Goes Cold
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pipes freeze before people do. Water lines in exterior walls, crawl spaces, unheated garages and under sinks on outside walls are at risk once indoor temperatures fall well below freezing near them, and a burst pipe can do more damage than the outage itself. Your warm-room plan protects people; it does not protect the plumbing in the rest of the house.
          </p>

          <p>
            In the early hours, open the cabinet doors under kitchen and bathroom sinks so room air reaches the pipes, and let faucets on exterior walls drip slowly, both hot and cold. Moving water is much harder to freeze, and an open faucet relieves pressure if ice does form. Know where your main water shutoff is and make sure it turns before you need it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the outage looks long and the house is dropping toward freezing, consider draining the system: shut off the main, open the lowest faucet and the highest, and flush toilets to empty tanks. Shut off power or gas to the water heater according to its manual before draining lines that feed it. This is the step plumbers recommend for vacant houses in winter, and it removes the risk entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not try to thaw a frozen pipe with an open flame or a propane heater pointed at it. Use warm towels or a hair dryer once power returns, and work from the faucet end back toward the frozen section so melting water has somewhere to go. If a pipe has already burst, shut off the main water before anything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="never-indoors" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. What Never Comes Inside
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every winter storm brings the same headlines, and the causes are the same list. Never use a portable generator inside a home, garage, basement, crawl space or shed, even with doors and windows open. Run it outside, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. A garage with the door up is not outside. Generators produce carbon monoxide fast enough to kill within minutes in an enclosed space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never use a charcoal or gas grill, a camp stove, a turkey fryer or an outdoor patio heater indoors for heat. None of them has an oxygen depletion sensor, and a charcoal grill in particular produces large amounts of CO with no visible smoke once the coals are lit. Never use a gas oven or range to heat the room; they are not designed for it, and an oven left open for hours can produce dangerous CO levels. Candles are a fire risk out of proportion to their heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Heaters sold for garages, workshops and construction sites, including forced-air propane torpedo heaters and many tank-top heaters, are designed for well-ventilated spaces and are labeled accordingly. The label decides. If a heater does not say it is approved for indoor residential use, treat it as an outdoor heater regardless of how it is marketed in a listing or a forum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fireplaces and wood stoves you already have are a different matter. If your chimney is inspected and the stove or fireplace is in good condition, it is an excellent outage heat source, but this is not the time to experiment with one you have not used in years. A cracked flue or a chimney fire in the middle of a storm, with emergency services stretched thin, is a disaster stacked on another one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="choosing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which One to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the alarm. Buy a battery-powered CO alarm such as the First Alert CO400 for every level of the house and every sleeping area, and one more for the room where you plan to run a heater. If you do nothing else on this list, do this. Then buy one cold-rated sleeping bag, like the Coleman North Rim, for each person in the household. Those two purchases cover the most dangerous and the most common outage problems without fuel or power.
          </p>

          <p>
            For actual heat, the Mr. Heater Portable Buddy is the right first heater for most homes. It warms a closed bedroom, runs on cylinders you can store for years, and has the safety devices that make indoor use reasonable. If your warm room is a living room or the household is large, choose the Big Buddy instead, and plan to run it on low or medium after the first warm-up. If you only need to take the chill off a bathroom or small bedroom, or want a second unit, the Little Buddy fits. Stock enough 1-lb cylinders for several days at a few hours of use a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own a power station, add a heated throw like Sunbeam's to the kit and plan to use stored power for people rather than rooms. If you own a generator or a large home battery, a small ceramic heater like the Lasko 754200 makes sense as clean electric heat for one room. On a typical portable station, skip it. And if you are still deciding how to back up the house, our guides to the <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">best home generators</a> and the <a href="/best-portable-chargers-for-power-outages/">best portable chargers for power outages</a> cover the rest of the kit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, write the plan down and practice it once before the cold arrives. Know which room you will use, where the cylinders and alarms are, how to shut off the water, and what temperature or forecast will send you to a warming center or a relative's house instead. The heater is the least important part of that plan. Knowing when to use it, and when to leave, matters more.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Any Heater Without a Battery CO Alarm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hardwired CO alarms without battery backup go quiet in an outage, which is exactly when fuel-burning heaters come inside. An indoor-rated heater is only part of the safety system. Put a battery-powered alarm in the same room and near every sleeping area before you light anything, and test it first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an Outdoor Heater, Grill or Generator Inside</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Patio heaters, tank-top heaters, torpedo heaters, grills, camp stoves and generators are not indoor appliances, even with a window open or the garage door up. They lack oxygen depletion sensors and can produce lethal carbon monoxide in minutes. If the label does not say indoor residential use, it stays outside.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Fresh-Air Opening</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Indoor-rated propane heaters still burn oxygen, and their manuals require a fresh-air opening of a specific size. Sealing the room tight to keep the heat in defeats the design and can trigger the oxygen sensor or worse. Crack the window or door by the amount the manual specifies, every time the heater runs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to Heat a Room From a Power Station</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,500-watt heater empties a 1kWh power station in roughly 35 to 40 minutes, and the room is cold again an hour later. Spend stored energy on heated blankets, which draw a small fraction as much, and on the fridge, lights and medical devices that cannot run on anything else.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Heating the Whole House Instead of One Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A small propane heater cannot keep up with an open-plan floor, so it burns through cylinders and the house still feels cold. Choose one small interior room, close it off, block drafts and hang blankets over windows. A Portable Buddy can make that room comfortable on a fraction of the fuel.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the safest indoor heater for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The safest options burn nothing at all: cold-rated sleeping bags, layered clothing and a heated blanket on a power station. For actual room heat without electricity, choose a propane heater the manufacturer rates for indoor use, such as the Mr. Heater Buddy series, which has an oxygen depletion sensor and tip-over shutoff. Always pair it with a battery-powered CO alarm and the ventilation its manual requires.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Mr. Heater Buddy really safe to use indoors?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mr. Heater markets the Buddy series as indoor-safe, and the heaters include an oxygen depletion sensor and a tip-over shutoff. That makes indoor use reasonable when you follow the manual: provide the specified fresh-air opening, keep the stated clearance from combustibles, use 1-lb cylinders indoors, and run a working battery CO alarm in the same room. Many safety agencies advise turning it off before sleeping.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a 1-lb propane cylinder last in a Buddy heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 1-lb cylinder holds a little over 21,000 BTU of energy. At 9,000 BTU an hour that works out to roughly two to two and a half hours, and at 4,000 BTU about five hours. The 3,800 BTU Little Buddy runs about five to six hours. Cold weather and partly used cylinders shorten that, so stock more cylinders than the math suggests.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a space heater on a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can, but not for long. A 1,500-watt heater drains a 1kWh station in roughly 35 to 40 minutes, and even a low setting only stretches that to about an hour. That energy is better spent on a heated blanket, which can run most of the night on the same battery. Space heaters make sense on generators or large whole-home batteries.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I heat my house without electricity?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Concentrate on one small room. Close it off, block drafts, cover windows and gather everyone there. Use an indoor-rated propane heater with a CO alarm and the required ventilation during waking hours, and let cold-rated sleeping bags and layered clothing do the work overnight. An existing, inspected wood stove or fireplace is also a good option if it is in sound condition.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use my gas oven or stove to heat the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Gas ovens and ranges are not designed as space heaters, and running one for hours with the door open can produce dangerous levels of carbon monoxide and creates a fire risk. The same goes for camp stoves and grills of every kind. Use only heaters labeled for indoor residential use, and keep a battery CO alarm working whenever anything burns inside.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a kerosene heater for power outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We left kerosene heaters off this list. Whether they may be used indoors depends on state and local rules, they require careful fuel storage and refueling outdoors, and they need generous ventilation. For most households, an indoor-rated propane heater with 1-lb cylinders is simpler to store and use safely. If you consider kerosene, check your local codes and the manual first.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

